VS folder structure, where to put:
requirements.txt
config.json

In the VS Code terminal, from your project root:
source .venv/bin/activate
On macOS/Linux, your prompt should then change to something like:
(.venv) time-controlled-queries-demo %
Verify it with:
which python
It should point to:
.../time-controlled-queries-demo/.venv/bin/python
If .venv does not exist yet, create it first:
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate

update requirements.txt

For the code we’ve written so far, requirements.txt can stay empty because we’re using only the Python standard library.
I’d make it:

# No external Python dependencies required.
If you want to switch the API calls from urllib to the more convenient requests library, then use:
requests==2.32.3
and install it with:
pip install -r requirements.txt
For this demo, I’d keep it dependency-free for now.

for the demo, job 1 should call https://invest.wofuer.com/myapi/health

For the demo, let's make Job 1 call your health endpoint. I’d also start evolving config.json toward supporting multiple jobs.
config/config.json
{
  "api": {
    "requests_per_minute": 10,
    "timeout_seconds": 30
  },
  "jobs": [
    {
      "id": "job-1",
      "name": "API Health Check",
      "url": "https://invest.wofuer.com/myapi/health",
      "method": "GET",
      "delay_seconds": 30,
      "enabled": true
    }
  ],
  "runtime": {
    "throttle_file": "runtime/throttle.json"
  }
}